For Reply A, the rule requires the answer {"c": 3}, because the last object inside any code fence is {"c": 3} in the second fence. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer {"e": 5}, because the last object inside any code fence is {"e": 5} in the second fence. My conjecture is that the check returns {"a": 1} for both replies, because the second fenced candidate is the whole span between the second pair of fences, which contains two adjacent JSON objects and therefore fails to parse as a single object; the code then falls through to the first fenced candidate, which parses and is returned.

